WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.992 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.992 --> 00:00:03.984 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.984 --> 00:00:05.976 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.976 --> 00:00:18.260 align:middle line:90%


00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:19.280 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: OK.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.880 align:middle line:84%
Tarek has just
joined us, I believe.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:23.640 align:middle line:90%
TAREK MANSOUR: Hey, everyone.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.400 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Hey, Tarek.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:26.840 align:middle line:90%
Well, welcome.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:30.280 align:middle line:84%
And just as a
brief introduction,

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.160 align:middle line:84%
I'm really very
proud to introduce

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.280 align:middle line:90%
Tarek Mansour to everyone.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:43.480 align:middle line:84%
He graduated from MIT about six
years ago or seven years ago,

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.580 align:middle line:84%
and he was actually
in this class.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:50.360 align:middle line:84%
And so we're really
very pleased that he

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.580 align:middle line:90%
has come as a guest lecturer.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:56.420 align:middle line:84%
And his background, he
left-- when he left MIT,

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:02.790 align:middle line:84%
he joined really the giants
of Wall Street, Goldman Sachs,

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.150 align:middle line:90%
Citadel, and Bridgewater.

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:08.870 align:middle line:84%
I'm sure most of you are very
familiar with those names.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:14.630 align:middle line:84%
And three years ago
he started Kalshi

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:21.150 align:middle line:84%
with Luana Lopes Lara,
another MIT student.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:24.630 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's been a
phenomenal success, especially

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:26.110 align:middle line:90%
in the last month.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:31.710 align:middle line:84%
So let me hand it over to
Tarek to share his experiences

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:35.237 align:middle line:90%
and details on Kalshi.

00:01:35.237 --> 00:01:37.570 align:middle line:84%
TAREK MANSOUR: Well, first
of all, thanks for having me,

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:38.670 align:middle line:90%
Peter.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:41.450 align:middle line:84%
Really, really excited
to be back in the class.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:44.390 align:middle line:84%
I know Lara did last
year, and I think

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.950 align:middle line:90%
I did the year before that.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:51.457 align:middle line:84%
And maybe I'll talk a
little bit about background,

00:01:51.457 --> 00:01:53.790 align:middle line:84%
leading-- the way I'm thinking
about doing this is just,

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.110 align:middle line:84%
I'll talk a little about
the story of Kalshi,

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.630 align:middle line:84%
how we decided to do it, what
is the long term vision, what

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.470 align:middle line:90%
we're trying to achieve.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:02.310 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll do a
little bit of Q&A,

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:05.110 align:middle line:84%
and then maybe we'll see
how it goes from there.

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:07.750 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Excuse me,
Tarek, just a quick question.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:11.930 align:middle line:90%
Your video isn't on for us.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:13.450 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if
you can turn it on.

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:15.270 align:middle line:90%
That would be great.

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.790 align:middle line:84%
TAREK MANSOUR: It should
be on, but let me check.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:19.040 align:middle line:90%
It's on for me.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:20.290 align:middle line:90%
It's on Zoom, so let me check.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:21.123 align:middle line:90%
Let me double check.

00:02:21.123 --> 00:02:22.710 align:middle line:90%
One second.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:25.150 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE:
Oh, that's great.

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:27.568 align:middle line:90%
We can see you now.

00:02:27.568 --> 00:02:28.610 align:middle line:90%
TAREK MANSOUR: Oh, great.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:29.390 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:30.130 align:middle line:90%
Sorry about that.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:35.790 align:middle line:84%
I think I'm in a little
bit of a unstable Wi-Fi.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:37.870 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:40.170 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll talk a little
bit about my background.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:43.330 align:middle line:84%
I really like coming
back to this class

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:48.790 align:middle line:84%
because a lot of the interest
in financial markets and math,

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:50.130 align:middle line:90%
I think, stem from the class.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:52.450 align:middle line:90%
So I was born in California.

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:53.790 align:middle line:90%
I grew up in Lebanon.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:57.500 align:middle line:84%
I think one of the
things that I really

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:00.912 align:middle line:84%
enjoyed doing in middle
school and high school is,

00:03:00.912 --> 00:03:02.120 align:middle line:90%
I actually really loved math.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.270 align:middle line:84%
I really, really
liked math, and I

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:06.020 align:middle line:84%
think it felt like MIT
was the right place

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:12.720 align:middle line:84%
to go if-- for someone who
loves math, and so got into MIT.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.820 align:middle line:84%
And I think the thing I
realized is one of the best

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.860 align:middle line:84%
applications of math
is finance, to start

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:21.338 align:middle line:90%
working in a number of--

00:03:21.338 --> 00:03:22.880 align:middle line:84%
whether it was sell
side or buy side,

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:25.020 align:middle line:90%
so banks like Goldman Sachs.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.000 align:middle line:90%
I worked at Citadel.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.580 align:middle line:90%
I worked at small prop shops.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:33.400 align:middle line:84%
And then the thing
I realized is--

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:34.920 align:middle line:84%
I would say, I
realized two things.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:37.280 align:middle line:90%
I mean, one, I like trading.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:42.080 align:middle line:84%
I really enjoy trading, but I
realized I prefer building more.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:45.420 align:middle line:84%
I like the notion of
building new products

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.420 align:middle line:84%
and building something
new, but it's so tangible.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:53.160 align:middle line:84%
And two, when I was
at Goldman, I mean,

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:59.410 align:middle line:84%
I think a lot of the
thing that we've seen

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:01.860 align:middle line:84%
is that financial instruments
do get developed over time.

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:03.610 align:middle line:84%
They take a long period
of time, but there

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.590 align:middle line:84%
is a lot of opportunities to
basically develop new things.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:07.632 align:middle line:84%
And there's always needs
for a lot of new things.

00:04:07.632 --> 00:04:09.490 align:middle line:84%
One example in 2016 is
that a lot of people

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:13.170 align:middle line:84%
were actually trying to get a
hedge or an exposure to Brexit,

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.138 align:middle line:84%
and they didn't really--
they weren't really

00:04:15.138 --> 00:04:16.430 align:middle line:90%
the right instruments for that.

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:18.555 align:middle line:84%
People would use traditional
financial instruments,

00:04:18.555 --> 00:04:20.890 align:middle line:84%
like stocks and
options and others,

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:24.690 align:middle line:84%
to get that binary exposure
that people were looking for.

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.130 align:middle line:90%
And it was just not perfect.

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:27.630 align:middle line:90%
It was not.

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:28.350 align:middle line:90%
It was a proxy.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.050 align:middle line:84%
It was indirect, and
it was super expensive

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:35.610 align:middle line:84%
because it was what we call
over-the-counter, OTC traded.

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:38.635 align:middle line:84%
And so what we thought, and
it was kind of really early

00:04:38.635 --> 00:04:40.010 align:middle line:84%
at the time was,
like, well, what

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:41.470 align:middle line:90%
if we have a financial market?

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:43.262 align:middle line:84%
So we have a financial
market for equities,

00:04:43.262 --> 00:04:45.690 align:middle line:84%
so the price equities,
one that prices credit,

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:48.950 align:middle line:84%
like bonds and so on; one
that prices commodities;

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:51.062 align:middle line:84%
and we have financial
markets for currencies.

00:04:51.062 --> 00:04:53.520 align:middle line:84%
What if we had a financial
market that prices basically any

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.480 align:middle line:84%
question about the
future, any event that

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.200 align:middle line:84%
is in the horizon that is
meaningful economically

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.760 align:middle line:90%
or socially or culturally?

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.600 align:middle line:84%
And I think the thing
that became very apparent

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:09.880 align:middle line:84%
is, one, if such a market were
to exist, it has the potential

00:05:09.880 --> 00:05:12.907 align:middle line:84%
to be the biggest
financial market of all,

00:05:12.907 --> 00:05:15.240 align:middle line:84%
mostly because it's just
broader than anything else that

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:16.760 align:middle line:90%
came before it.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:21.160 align:middle line:84%
And two, there were a
lot of regulatory issues

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:24.213 align:middle line:90%
with that type of market.

00:05:24.213 --> 00:05:25.880 align:middle line:84%
Basically, historically,
the US has made

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:27.940 align:middle line:90%
these illegal or unregulated.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:29.800 align:middle line:90%
They haven't let anyone do it.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.200 align:middle line:84%
And at the time we
were very naive.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:38.600 align:middle line:84%
We thought, well, that sounds
like a problem we can solve.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:42.920 align:middle line:84%
And we just got into it, so
we decided to start a company.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:46.920 align:middle line:84%
I didn't go back to
Citadel, and so we just

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:48.420 align:middle line:90%
decided to start Kalshi.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.920 align:middle line:84%
And the premise is really like--
it's like, how about-- what

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:53.305 align:middle line:84%
if we build the first
large-scale prediction market,

00:05:53.305 --> 00:05:55.180 align:middle line:84%
and we want to do it as
a financial exchange,

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:58.280 align:middle line:84%
a regulated financial exchange
so that this can become an asset

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:00.640 align:middle line:84%
class that's traded one
day by the large hedge

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:05.360 align:middle line:84%
funds and institutions and
brokers and so on and so forth?

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.740 align:middle line:84%
And when we first
started, it was tough.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:12.920 align:middle line:90%


00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:14.780 align:middle line:84%
Well, any company is
really tough to start,

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:17.985 align:middle line:84%
but I think this was
particularly tough because we--

00:06:17.985 --> 00:06:19.360 align:middle line:84%
it was kind of a
funny story, was

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:22.040 align:middle line:84%
we called pretty much every
single securities/derivatives

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:24.040 align:middle line:84%
lawyer in the country
and asked them, hey,

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.580 align:middle line:90%
we're trying to do this.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:28.280 align:middle line:90%
Can you help us?

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:30.960 align:middle line:84%
And the answer was basically,
everybody-- the range

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.560 align:middle line:84%
was either being laughed out
of the room, all the way to,

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:36.600 align:middle line:84%
hey, this is just
not going to work.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:39.440 align:middle line:84%
The CFTC and the
regulators just never

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:41.120 align:middle line:90%
wanted to do anything like this.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:45.840 align:middle line:84%
And the reason we kept going
is because none of the answers

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.740 align:middle line:84%
are actually
intellectually satisfying.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.190 align:middle line:90%
There wasn't a clear answer.

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:49.890 align:middle line:90%
Like, OK, why?

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:53.958 align:middle line:84%
They're like, well, because
of they could be manipulated.

00:06:53.958 --> 00:06:55.750 align:middle line:84%
I'm like, OK, but that's
solvable, same way

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:57.280 align:middle line:90%
we solved the stock market.

00:06:57.280 --> 00:06:59.530 align:middle line:84%
And then the other one is,
well, it could be gambling.

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:01.572 align:middle line:84%
It's like, well, why is
it gambling and something

00:07:01.572 --> 00:07:04.390 align:middle line:90%
like a grain future is not?

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:06.605 align:middle line:84%
I mean, some people are
betting on grain futures.

00:07:06.605 --> 00:07:08.730 align:middle line:84%
And sure, some people are
hedging on grain futures,

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:09.830 align:middle line:84%
but it's the same
thing for Brexit.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:11.630 align:middle line:84%
A lot of people are
exposed to Brexit.

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:14.210 align:middle line:90%
And we kept going.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:17.663 align:middle line:84%
We basically got the
company regulated.

00:07:17.663 --> 00:07:19.830 align:middle line:84%
We took three years, so we
took two years and a half

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:21.890 align:middle line:84%
to three to basically
get it regulated.

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:24.670 align:middle line:84%
And we basically launched
the first legal exchange

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:27.510 align:middle line:84%
or legal prediction market
in the US after that.

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:28.770 align:middle line:90%
It took us a very long time.

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:30.250 align:middle line:90%
It was a very long journey.

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:31.250 align:middle line:90%
But we got the exchange.

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:32.430 align:middle line:90%
We got the clearinghouse.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:36.950 align:middle line:84%
Well, we got the exchange at the
time, and we got up and running.

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:42.230 align:middle line:84%
And I think Peter has seen all
the journey of the company.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:47.360 align:middle line:84%
We started running and
then we grew a lot.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:49.660 align:middle line:84%
And then we had a
bunch of, I would say,

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:55.320 align:middle line:84%
issues and infighting between
us and the federal government,

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:58.563 align:middle line:84%
the CFTC, especially this
prior administration, which

00:07:58.563 --> 00:08:00.980 align:middle line:84%
was kind of really tough on
financial-- financial services

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:02.203 align:middle line:90%
and financial innovation.

00:08:02.203 --> 00:08:03.620 align:middle line:84%
And the big thing
that happened is

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:06.700 align:middle line:84%
they didn't let us list the
election market, which we always

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:12.642 align:middle line:84%
believe is one of the biggest
markets of all basically.

00:08:12.642 --> 00:08:14.600 align:middle line:84%
We took a very long time
to make that decision.

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:16.580 align:middle line:84%
It was a very tough
one, but we ended up

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:18.200 align:middle line:90%
suing the federal government.

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:21.580 align:middle line:84%
So we were really one of
those first, I would say,

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:26.500 align:middle line:84%
examples of a regulated company
suing its own regulator.

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:29.280 align:middle line:84%
It's a very tough and painful
decision to make at the time.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.100 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, it was the right one
now, looking back in retrospect.

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:36.539 align:middle line:84%
And we won earlier this
fall, and so that win

00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:38.260 align:middle line:84%
was actually the first
time in 100 years

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:39.860 align:middle line:84%
that you can actually
trade legally

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:41.539 align:middle line:90%
on the election in the US.

00:08:41.539 --> 00:08:43.220 align:middle line:84%
And that opens up a
lot of opportunities

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:46.500 align:middle line:84%
beyond the election because
what the lawsuit was about is,

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:48.740 align:middle line:84%
it's really expanding
the universe

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:52.700 align:middle line:84%
of things that are considered
financial instruments now.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:56.337 align:middle line:84%
And so where Kalshi
is at now, we've

00:08:56.337 --> 00:08:58.420 align:middle line:84%
grown a lot over the last
few years but definitely

00:08:58.420 --> 00:08:59.045 align:middle line:90%
the last month.

00:08:59.045 --> 00:09:02.980 align:middle line:90%


00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:06.040 align:middle line:84%
We've done over a billion of
volume in the last month alone.

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:12.460 align:middle line:84%
And now, as we think about
our future expansion plans,

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:15.260 align:middle line:84%
we're getting into a number
of different categories,

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:17.260 align:middle line:84%
financial services,
just basically

00:09:17.260 --> 00:09:19.580 align:middle line:90%
like financial markets, sports.

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:21.020 align:middle line:84%
We want to basically
have a market

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:23.562 align:middle line:84%
for every single piece of news
that's out there in the world.

00:09:23.562 --> 00:09:24.757 align:middle line:90%
And we want to over time.

00:09:24.757 --> 00:09:26.340 align:middle line:84%
What we're doing is,
we're integrating

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:29.690 align:middle line:84%
some of the large brokerages,
where we're going to have--

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:32.540 align:middle line:90%


00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:34.760 align:middle line:84%
people can come to kalshi.com
to trade the asset.

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:36.980 align:middle line:84%
But also if you want to
trade the asset from your own

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:39.600 align:middle line:84%
brokerage account, like see
your 401(k) in your stocks,

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:42.058 align:middle line:84%
you will be able to do that on
Schwab, Robinhood, Fidelity,

00:09:42.058 --> 00:09:44.410 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:48.390 align:middle line:84%
Let's talk a little bit about
finishing the narrative arc,

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:50.290 align:middle line:84%
and then I'll talk
some of personal stuff,

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:52.910 align:middle line:84%
going from being a student to
starting a company and so on.

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:57.930 align:middle line:84%
But one of the things that
was interesting throughout

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:02.490 align:middle line:84%
the journey is, a lot of what
it basically ends up taking

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:07.970 align:middle line:90%
is just perseverance around--

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:09.690 align:middle line:84%
basically perseverance
towards the goal

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:10.940 align:middle line:90%
that you're trying to achieve.

00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:14.555 align:middle line:90%


00:10:14.555 --> 00:10:16.570 align:middle line:84%
I mentioned at the
beginning that we were naive

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:17.590 align:middle line:90%
when we first started.

00:10:17.590 --> 00:10:19.423 align:middle line:84%
And in retrospect, I
think that was actually

00:10:19.423 --> 00:10:21.240 align:middle line:90%
one of the best things that--

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.490 align:middle line:84%
or I'd say one of the best
tools that we had, actually

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:26.890 align:middle line:84%
being naive, because a
lot of the people that--

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:29.450 align:middle line:84%
if we knew how hard it
would be, we probably just

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:30.842 align:middle line:90%
wouldn't have done it.

00:10:30.842 --> 00:10:33.550 align:middle line:84%
And there's something to be said
about when you climb a mountain,

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:36.450 align:middle line:84%
people tell you, just
look at the next step, one

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:37.450 align:middle line:90%
step at a time.

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:40.050 align:middle line:84%
Don't look at the summit,
just because when you look

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:41.920 align:middle line:90%
at the summit, you get tired.

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:44.200 align:middle line:84%
And I think there's
a lot to be said.

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:47.160 align:middle line:84%
I think this applies
to pretty much anything

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.080 align:middle line:90%
meaningful in life.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:50.080 align:middle line:84%
I think just doing anything
meaningful basically

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:52.960 align:middle line:90%
requires a lot of perseverance.

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:56.760 align:middle line:84%
The company has raised
more than $100 million

00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:03.360 align:middle line:84%
from a few investors, Sequoia,
KKR, Schwab itself, YC,

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:06.840 align:middle line:84%
and a bunch of others
in Silicon Valley.

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:09.340 align:middle line:90%
And I think that's about it.

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:10.850 align:middle line:90%
I think I covered most things.

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:13.100 align:middle line:84%
Peter, I was thinking we
could do a little bit of Q&A,

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:14.725 align:middle line:84%
and then we can orient
the conversation

00:11:14.725 --> 00:11:17.800 align:middle line:84%
to some of the places that the
students want to hear about.

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:18.920 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Yes.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:19.420 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:20.540 align:middle line:90%
Thank you, Tarek.

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:22.240 align:middle line:90%
And let's see with questions.

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:26.640 align:middle line:84%
Just so Tarek can hear you,
I'll hand you a microphone,

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:29.120 align:middle line:84%
or we'll pass a
microphone to you.

00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:30.100 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: One, two.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:30.600 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:33.800 align:middle line:84%
I don't know where
you can see me from.

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:37.960 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, this is
really fascinating.

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:40.430 align:middle line:84%
I have a question,
which is, what

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:43.970 align:middle line:84%
is the difference between
this and a betting market?

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:47.950 align:middle line:84%
To me it sounds the same, like
you're betting on an event.

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:51.430 align:middle line:84%
Because, surely, if someone
wanted to hedge against Brexit,

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:53.830 align:middle line:84%
they could just go
into a betting market

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:57.380 align:middle line:84%
and bet a certain quantity of
money on whether Brexit happens.

00:11:57.380 --> 00:12:00.710 align:middle line:90%


00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:03.430 align:middle line:84%
TAREK MANSOUR: I do think
there's a lot of similarities.

00:12:03.430 --> 00:12:04.710 align:middle line:84%
And I would say there's
a lot of similarities

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:06.910 align:middle line:84%
between betting markets and any
traditional financial markets,

00:12:06.910 --> 00:12:08.868 align:middle line:84%
a lot of people that are
speculating or betting

00:12:08.868 --> 00:12:11.710 align:middle line:84%
or gambling on a lot, really a
lot of markets, including crypto

00:12:11.710 --> 00:12:13.950 align:middle line:90%
and stocks and really anything.

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:16.930 align:middle line:84%
But I think it's a bit more
of a structural difference.

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:19.210 align:middle line:84%
First of all, this
is an exchange.

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:20.950 align:middle line:84%
People are trading
against each other.

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:21.650 align:middle line:90%
There's no book.

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:22.400 align:middle line:90%
There's no bookie.

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:23.790 align:middle line:90%
There's no concept of bookie.

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:24.870 align:middle line:84%
And actually, any
institution that

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:26.578 align:middle line:84%
wants to hedge against
Brexit would never

00:12:26.578 --> 00:12:28.950 align:middle line:84%
do it on a betting market,
just because the prices

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:30.812 align:middle line:90%
are set by the bookie.

00:12:30.812 --> 00:12:33.270 align:middle line:84%
They would do it probably with
an investment bank, which is

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:34.520 align:middle line:90%
what they're doing right now.

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:36.770 align:middle line:84%
But the prices are kind of
bad at the investment bank.

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:38.310 align:middle line:90%
It's over the counter.

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:40.653 align:middle line:84%
You negotiate bilaterally
with the investment bank

00:12:40.653 --> 00:12:42.070 align:middle line:84%
instead of just
basically doing it

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:44.653 align:middle line:84%
on exchange, where there's other
people that are independently

00:12:44.653 --> 00:12:47.873 align:middle line:84%
pricing the events so
that you can basically

00:12:47.873 --> 00:12:49.790 align:middle line:84%
know what the right price
is to basically make

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:51.870 align:middle line:90%
the transaction at.

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:53.870 align:middle line:84%
I think there are a
lot of similarities.

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:57.110 align:middle line:84%
And frankly, I haven't-- we
don't really discern them quite

00:12:57.110 --> 00:12:57.610 align:middle line:90%
much.

00:12:57.610 --> 00:12:59.318 align:middle line:84%
I think over time the
two are converging,

00:12:59.318 --> 00:13:01.932 align:middle line:84%
and they're going to end
up being one and the same.

00:13:01.932 --> 00:13:03.390 align:middle line:84%
The fundamental
difference, I would

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:06.330 align:middle line:84%
say, like from a regulatory
or legal perspective,

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:09.310 align:middle line:84%
but also like, why is it
the financial instrument?

00:13:09.310 --> 00:13:11.270 align:middle line:84%
is, are you trading
on natural risks,

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:13.333 align:middle line:84%
or are you trading
on artificial risks?

00:13:13.333 --> 00:13:14.750 align:middle line:84%
So when you go to
a casino and you

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:17.110 align:middle line:84%
roll a dice, that's
a risk that you're

00:13:17.110 --> 00:13:21.967 align:middle line:84%
creating for the purpose of
entertainment, whereas Brexit,

00:13:21.967 --> 00:13:23.550 align:middle line:84%
that's not a risk
that you're creating

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:24.925 align:middle line:84%
for the purpose
of entertainment.

00:13:24.925 --> 00:13:27.710 align:middle line:84%
That's a risk that exists that
some people have and they're

00:13:27.710 --> 00:13:29.960 align:middle line:84%
exposed to, and they may
want to basically transfer it

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:30.750 align:middle line:90%
to someone else.

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:32.630 align:middle line:84%
And so that's of the
delineation of why

00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:34.190 align:middle line:84%
this is a financial
market and why

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:39.415 align:middle line:84%
having it on a financial
market is appropriate.

00:13:39.415 --> 00:13:41.540 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, I mean, I think
the two main differences,

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:42.260 align:middle line:90%
it is an exchange.

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:43.000 align:middle line:90%
We have an order book.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:44.417 align:middle line:84%
People are trading
it the same way

00:13:44.417 --> 00:13:47.020 align:middle line:84%
as a stock market, the same way
they trade in a stock market.

00:13:47.020 --> 00:13:49.818 align:middle line:84%
And then, two, it's
risk that exists.

00:13:49.818 --> 00:13:52.360 align:middle line:84%
And so people can basically do
what they want with that risk,

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:54.818 align:middle line:84%
whether they want to get exposed
to it more by speculating,

00:13:54.818 --> 00:13:57.740 align:middle line:84%
or they want to expose-- get
exposed to it less by hedging.

00:13:57.740 --> 00:13:58.680 align:middle line:90%
It's a great question.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:13:59.347 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Thanks.

00:13:59.347 --> 00:14:01.788 align:middle line:84%
Can I just ask a quick
follow up, which is--

00:14:01.788 --> 00:14:03.580 align:middle line:84%
and this has just come
to my mind, so maybe

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:04.860 align:middle line:90%
a silly question--

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:07.380 align:middle line:84%
has there been any
arbitrage opportunities

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:11.833 align:middle line:84%
between the betting
markets and this exchange?

00:14:11.833 --> 00:14:13.500 align:middle line:84%
TAREK MANSOUR: Yeah,
I mean, well, we're

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:15.700 align:middle line:84%
the only legal one in the
US, so there's kind of--

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:16.860 align:middle line:90%
I mean, in terms of--

00:14:16.860 --> 00:14:19.660 align:middle line:84%
there's more
arbitrage between us

00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:21.080 align:middle line:84%
and traditional
financial assets.

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:23.455 align:middle line:84%
So with the election, for
example, people were arbitrage.

00:14:23.455 --> 00:14:26.580 align:middle line:84%
A lot of hedge funds were
arbitraging the election market

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:33.550 align:middle line:84%
with other assets like
crypto or S&P and so on.

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:36.030 align:middle line:90%
And that, we see a lot of that.

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:36.530 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: OK.

00:14:36.530 --> 00:14:37.470 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that makes sense.

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:38.230 align:middle line:90%
That's fascinating.

00:14:38.230 --> 00:14:38.750 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:47.515 align:middle line:90%


00:14:47.515 --> 00:14:49.890 align:middle line:84%
TAREK MANSOUR: Steven, I don't
know if you can give him--

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:53.770 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if you can
unmute yourself or--

00:14:53.770 --> 00:14:54.270 align:middle line:90%
oh, sorry.

00:14:54.270 --> 00:14:55.810 align:middle line:90%
What's the question?

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:58.050 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Can you hear me?

00:14:58.050 --> 00:14:59.450 align:middle line:90%
TAREK MANSOUR: Yep.

00:14:59.450 --> 00:15:01.450 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Yeah.

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:04.470 align:middle line:84%
I'm also interested in
careers in trading as well,

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:05.910 align:middle line:84%
and I find it
fascinating as well.

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:09.610 align:middle line:84%
So it's good to hear that
you were interested in that

00:15:09.610 --> 00:15:10.970 align:middle line:90%
and did that as a career.

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:15.410 align:middle line:84%
But I was just wondering, how
did your experiences working

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:18.250 align:middle line:90%
in trading help you to--

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:22.490 align:middle line:84%
or if those skills helped you
with what you pursued at Kalshi

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:23.070 align:middle line:90%
or not?

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:25.850 align:middle line:90%


00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:30.430 align:middle line:84%
TAREK MANSOUR: I would
say maybe a few things.

00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:33.830 align:middle line:84%
So one, I mean, it's always
good to get first-hand exposure

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:35.330 align:middle line:84%
into how things are
done in a market

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:40.970 align:middle line:84%
that you're trying to
be-- trying to disrupt.

00:15:40.970 --> 00:15:41.550 align:middle line:90%
It's good.

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:43.550 align:middle line:84%
You have to have a
reasonably good understanding

00:15:43.550 --> 00:15:44.358 align:middle line:90%
of how things work.

00:15:44.358 --> 00:15:46.150 align:middle line:84%
And the thing I'd say
is, it's interesting.

00:15:46.150 --> 00:15:49.970 align:middle line:84%
If you stay in an
industry for too long,

00:15:49.970 --> 00:15:52.110 align:middle line:84%
it actually gets a bit
harder to disrupt it.

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:55.570 align:middle line:84%
You get too conditioned
to how things are done,

00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:58.930 align:middle line:90%
and maybe you get--

00:15:58.930 --> 00:16:01.010 align:middle line:84%
maybe it gets harder
for you to become--

00:16:01.010 --> 00:16:04.290 align:middle line:84%
to have any degree of
naivete, which I think

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:05.390 align:middle line:90%
is not a very good thing.

00:16:05.390 --> 00:16:06.932 align:middle line:84%
But I think some
degree of experience

00:16:06.932 --> 00:16:09.690 align:middle line:84%
is good so that you have a
baseline understanding of what

00:16:09.690 --> 00:16:11.913 align:middle line:84%
your end customer-- how
your end customer basically

00:16:11.913 --> 00:16:14.330 align:middle line:84%
thinks about the world and how
they use different products

00:16:14.330 --> 00:16:16.730 align:middle line:90%
and adopt them.

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:19.770 align:middle line:84%
The second thing I
think is, I would

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:21.672 align:middle line:84%
say I learned a lot from
the Citadel culture,

00:16:21.672 --> 00:16:23.630 align:middle line:84%
maybe less from Goldman
and some of the others,

00:16:23.630 --> 00:16:27.730 align:middle line:84%
but Citadel, I really
enjoyed working at Citadel.

00:16:27.730 --> 00:16:30.160 align:middle line:84%
I really like a
lot of the aspects

00:16:30.160 --> 00:16:32.240 align:middle line:84%
of the culture and the
way that things are done.

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:34.400 align:middle line:84%
And I would say,
it was interesting

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:42.560 align:middle line:84%
because I think citadel had this
ultimate version of, let's call

00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:45.600 align:middle line:90%
it performance-driven culture.

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:47.860 align:middle line:84%
And what I mean by that is,
it's a full meritocracy.

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:50.240 align:middle line:84%
It's very little
management layers.

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:52.960 align:middle line:84%
Even if you're young, they
will give you a lot of ability

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:56.640 align:middle line:90%
to take risks and do big things.

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:59.520 align:middle line:84%
And they're uniquely fit to
do that because in trading,

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:03.020 align:middle line:84%
something that's very unique to
trading is that you have a P&L.

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:06.398 align:middle line:84%
It's undeniable whether
you're doing well versus not.

00:17:06.398 --> 00:17:07.940 align:middle line:84%
If you make money,
you're doing well.

00:17:07.940 --> 00:17:09.760 align:middle line:90%
If you're not, you're not.

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:13.040 align:middle line:84%
Whereas in tech and others,
there's a lot of politics.

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:14.460 align:middle line:90%
The boss likes you versus not.

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:16.880 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there's just a
lot-- a lot of noise.

00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:20.000 align:middle line:84%
And I think we try to bring that
to the culture of the company

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:21.020 align:middle line:90%
as much as we can.

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:22.260 align:middle line:90%
We're very flat as a company.

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:26.180 align:middle line:84%
We have a lot of people that
are a few years out of college.

00:17:26.180 --> 00:17:27.380 align:middle line:90%
A lot of them are from MIT.

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:31.370 align:middle line:84%
They're running huge, huge
chunks of the company already

00:17:31.370 --> 00:17:33.350 align:middle line:90%
and with teams and so on.

00:17:33.350 --> 00:17:35.333 align:middle line:84%
And just because for
us, it's very similar.

00:17:35.333 --> 00:17:36.750 align:middle line:84%
If you do well,
you basically grow

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:39.010 align:middle line:84%
extremely fast within a few
months within the company.

00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:41.030 align:middle line:90%
And if not, then not.

00:17:41.030 --> 00:17:46.250 align:middle line:84%
And that comes a bit from the
trading culture, I would say.

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:46.750 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: OK.

00:17:46.750 --> 00:17:46.910 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:47.618 align:middle line:90%
That makes sense.

00:17:47.618 --> 00:17:48.130 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:17:48.130 --> 00:17:57.070 align:middle line:90%


00:17:57.070 --> 00:17:59.870 align:middle line:84%
JAKE XIA: I can
field you a question.

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:00.890 align:middle line:90%
Hey, Tarek.

00:18:00.890 --> 00:18:03.298 align:middle line:84%
Good to see you
back in the class.

00:18:03.298 --> 00:18:04.590 align:middle line:90%
TAREK MANSOUR: Good seeing you.

00:18:04.590 --> 00:18:05.673 align:middle line:90%
JAKE XIA: Good to see you.

00:18:05.673 --> 00:18:08.990 align:middle line:84%
So can you talk a little
bit about your margin

00:18:08.990 --> 00:18:14.030 align:middle line:84%
requirement and also maybe
central clearing, I guess?

00:18:14.030 --> 00:18:18.030 align:middle line:84%
You have two parties taking the
opposite side of a contract.

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:21.150 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the payout
that says 100%,

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:26.480 align:middle line:84%
they may have paid a percentage
of that total payout.

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:28.580 align:middle line:84%
When the market moves,
do you require them

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:31.100 align:middle line:90%
to post the variation margin?

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:34.820 align:middle line:90%
How do you monitor that?

00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:35.660 align:middle line:90%
TAREK MANSOUR: Yeah.

00:18:35.660 --> 00:18:37.690 align:middle line:84%
So we are fully
cash collateralized

00:18:37.690 --> 00:18:39.940 align:middle line:84%
right now, so what it means
is people put the money up

00:18:39.940 --> 00:18:43.182 align:middle line:90%
front for their positions.

00:18:43.182 --> 00:18:45.140 align:middle line:84%
And then we basically
mark to market every day.

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:47.182 align:middle line:84%
Actually, we mark to market
on a real-time basis,

00:18:47.182 --> 00:18:50.620 align:middle line:90%
so we do it in real time.

00:18:50.620 --> 00:18:52.880 align:middle line:84%
We are going to introduce
leverage next year, I think,

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:54.630 align:middle line:84%
and that's going to
be pretty interesting.

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:57.657 align:middle line:84%
We're thinking about what our
margin model is going to be.

00:18:57.657 --> 00:18:59.240 align:middle line:84%
And maybe for the
class, I'll explain.

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:02.540 align:middle line:84%
So in a lot of
financial markets, you--

00:19:02.540 --> 00:19:05.620 align:middle line:84%
what people consider margin
leverage is when you buy--

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:09.300 align:middle line:84%
when hedge funds
basically buy, let's say,

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:13.580 align:middle line:84%
interest rate futures
or S&P future.

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:16.280 align:middle line:84%
Let's say they want to buy like
$1 million worth of S&P futures.

00:19:16.280 --> 00:19:18.552 align:middle line:84%
They don't put up $1 million
with the clearinghouse

00:19:18.552 --> 00:19:19.260 align:middle line:90%
and the exchange.

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:23.320 align:middle line:84%
They put up, let's say, $100,000
or $10,000, which would be 10%,

00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:25.100 align:middle line:90%
1% in margin.

00:19:25.100 --> 00:19:28.100 align:middle line:84%
And then as the thing
moves over time,

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:29.780 align:middle line:84%
the clearinghouse
asks you to-- if you

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:31.560 align:middle line:84%
made a little bit of money,
the clearinghouse pays you.

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:33.180 align:middle line:84%
And if you lost,
the clearinghouse

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:36.580 align:middle line:84%
asks you to put up a bit more
margin to cover your losses.

00:19:36.580 --> 00:19:39.020 align:middle line:90%
And it goes like that over time.

00:19:39.020 --> 00:19:41.140 align:middle line:84%
Kalshi is a simpler
model where we actually

00:19:41.140 --> 00:19:42.920 align:middle line:90%
ask for 100% upfront.

00:19:42.920 --> 00:19:45.417 align:middle line:84%
We tell everyone, hey, give
us all the money upfront

00:19:45.417 --> 00:19:47.000 align:middle line:84%
so that there's no
risk in the system,

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:49.580 align:middle line:84%
and you're always sure
that anything you lose

00:19:49.580 --> 00:19:50.840 align:middle line:90%
is what you lose upfront.

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:53.642 align:middle line:84%
What you put up upfront, you
cannot lose more than that.

00:19:53.642 --> 00:19:56.100 align:middle line:84%
Over time, we're going to start
introducing leverage, which

00:19:56.100 --> 00:19:59.220 align:middle line:90%
is, we don't take 100% upfront.

00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:01.275 align:middle line:84%
And that's what Jake
was asking about.

00:20:01.275 --> 00:20:02.900 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's a
complicated-- that's

00:20:02.900 --> 00:20:05.020 align:middle line:84%
a complicated thing because
regulators are becoming very

00:20:05.020 --> 00:20:06.240 align:middle line:90%
strict about how we do this.

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:08.657 align:middle line:84%
And we have to be very-- we
need to make sure that nothing

00:20:08.657 --> 00:20:11.062 align:middle line:90%
like 2008 can happen again.

00:20:11.062 --> 00:20:13.020 align:middle line:84%
And so there's a lot of
requirements around how

00:20:13.020 --> 00:20:15.260 align:middle line:90%
this needs to be done.

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:20.900 align:middle line:84%
And so we expect to introduce
maybe margin like end

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:23.770 align:middle line:90%
of next year here potentially.

00:20:23.770 --> 00:20:26.170 align:middle line:90%
And we'll see how that goes.

00:20:26.170 --> 00:20:28.127 align:middle line:84%
We do have some products
where, for example,

00:20:28.127 --> 00:20:30.210 align:middle line:84%
if there's a lot of strong
correlation between two

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:33.530 align:middle line:84%
products, we return some
collateral to the participant.

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:36.210 align:middle line:84%
And we've already started
doing some of that.

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:38.113 align:middle line:84%
But over time we'll
do more and more.

00:20:38.113 --> 00:20:39.280 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, it's a great question.

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:43.570 align:middle line:90%


00:20:43.570 --> 00:20:46.930 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Yeah, so I
have a question on how

00:20:46.930 --> 00:20:50.130 align:middle line:84%
the projections for the company
have changed since the election.

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:52.745 align:middle line:84%
I assume there's a lot more
volume going through Kalshi

00:20:52.745 --> 00:20:53.870 align:middle line:90%
during the election period.

00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:55.250 align:middle line:84%
Have you guys been
able to retain

00:20:55.250 --> 00:20:58.130 align:middle line:84%
a lot of that trading volume,
or did you see a huge falloff?

00:20:58.130 --> 00:21:00.690 align:middle line:84%
And how did just the
election in general

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:03.307 align:middle line:84%
change where you see
the company going?

00:21:03.307 --> 00:21:05.390 align:middle line:84%
TAREK MANSOUR: Yeah, I
mean, I think the election,

00:21:05.390 --> 00:21:06.790 align:middle line:84%
so it's obviously
it's super huge.

00:21:06.790 --> 00:21:08.832 align:middle line:84%
But it's interesting
because our volume right now

00:21:08.832 --> 00:21:11.570 align:middle line:84%
is actually higher than it
was in the days leading up

00:21:11.570 --> 00:21:12.430 align:middle line:90%
to the election.

00:21:12.430 --> 00:21:14.710 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's
how exchanges work.

00:21:14.710 --> 00:21:17.490 align:middle line:84%
Once you acquire a lot,
acquire and onboard

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:19.930 align:middle line:84%
and open accounts for
customers, they kind of retain.

00:21:19.930 --> 00:21:20.702 align:middle line:90%
They stick.

00:21:20.702 --> 00:21:21.660 align:middle line:90%
They do other products.

00:21:21.660 --> 00:21:24.240 align:middle line:84%
Now we're seeing a lot
of uptick in crypto.

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:26.640 align:middle line:84%
We're seeing a lot of upticks
in our economics products.

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:28.700 align:middle line:84%
We're seeing uptick on
a lot of the politics,

00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:30.000 align:middle line:84%
like who's going to be
in the cabinet, who's

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:32.320 align:middle line:84%
going to-- there's all these
questions about, what's

00:21:32.320 --> 00:21:35.200 align:middle line:84%
government going to look
like in the next year?

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:37.720 align:middle line:84%
And then you have a lot of
crazy categories like weather.

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:40.840 align:middle line:84%
People come and are very
active on daily weather

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:43.120 align:middle line:84%
markets and then entertainment
markets and so on.

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:47.400 align:middle line:84%
So we see a lot of opportunities
across all these categories.

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:49.160 align:middle line:84%
And yeah, I mean,
I don't think--

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:52.000 align:middle line:84%
so it's not going to be
the same levels that we've

00:21:52.000 --> 00:21:54.140 align:middle line:84%
seen in the two days
leading up to the election,

00:21:54.140 --> 00:21:57.800 align:middle line:84%
but I think we're at a much
higher level than what we

00:21:57.800 --> 00:21:59.960 align:middle line:90%
were prior to the election now.

00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:04.560 align:middle line:84%
Think of it as a huge marketing
moment for the company.

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:07.420 align:middle line:84%
And that enabled the
product to go mainstream.

00:22:07.420 --> 00:22:14.956 align:middle line:90%


00:22:14.956 --> 00:22:16.120 align:middle line:90%
VASILY STRELA: Hey, Tarek.

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:17.600 align:middle line:90%
It's Vasily.

00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:19.740 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good to see you in class.

00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:20.428 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:22:20.428 --> 00:22:21.720 align:middle line:90%
TAREK MANSOUR: Good seeing you.

00:22:21.720 --> 00:22:22.920 align:middle line:90%
VASILY STRELA: Yeah.

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:23.860 align:middle line:90%
A question.

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:27.680 align:middle line:84%
So can you reveal
what's the percentage

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:32.800 align:middle line:84%
between institutional clients
and individuals on Kalshi

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:35.352 align:middle line:90%
at the moment?

00:22:35.352 --> 00:22:37.560 align:middle line:84%
TAREK MANSOUR: Right now
it's the breakdown of volume

00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:40.770 align:middle line:84%
is 30, 40% institutional,
and the rest is retail.

00:22:40.770 --> 00:22:43.760 align:middle line:90%


00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:50.480 align:middle line:84%
And I expect it to basically
go towards 50/50 over time.

00:22:50.480 --> 00:22:51.280 align:middle line:90%
It's interesting.

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:53.260 align:middle line:84%
Institutions do a
lot more volume,

00:22:53.260 --> 00:22:56.360 align:middle line:84%
but you can charge them
less; whereas retail,

00:22:56.360 --> 00:22:58.900 align:middle line:84%
like individual customers,
they do less volume.

00:22:58.900 --> 00:23:00.440 align:middle line:90%
They can be charged much more.

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:03.960 align:middle line:84%
And historically,
people have seen

00:23:03.960 --> 00:23:06.360 align:middle line:84%
the business model of being
kind of institutional first.

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:08.660 align:middle line:84%
The large exchanges
like NASDAQ or CME,

00:23:08.660 --> 00:23:11.902 align:middle line:90%
they're mostly institutional.

00:23:11.902 --> 00:23:13.860 align:middle line:84%
But I think it was kind
of a strategic mistake,

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:15.360 align:middle line:84%
and I think they're
waking up to it.

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:18.470 align:middle line:84%
They're realizing like, oh, God,
people on Reddit and Twitter

00:23:18.470 --> 00:23:21.456 align:middle line:84%
and average people now
hold a lot of the power.

00:23:21.456 --> 00:23:23.630 align:middle line:84%
They can decide that
a company like NVIDIA

00:23:23.630 --> 00:23:26.350 align:middle line:84%
is going to be worth a
few trillion dollars.

00:23:26.350 --> 00:23:28.450 align:middle line:90%
And that wasn't possible before.

00:23:28.450 --> 00:23:29.770 align:middle line:90%
People are richer now.

00:23:29.770 --> 00:23:32.835 align:middle line:84%
People are more involved
in the financial market,

00:23:32.835 --> 00:23:34.210 align:middle line:84%
and they're less
price-sensitive.

00:23:34.210 --> 00:23:35.950 align:middle line:84%
And so you see
companies like Coinbase

00:23:35.950 --> 00:23:38.030 align:middle line:90%
are doing incredibly well.

00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:39.310 align:middle line:90%
And yes, they have much less.

00:23:39.310 --> 00:23:41.870 align:middle line:84%
Coinbase's volume on a yearly
basis is around, I think,

00:23:41.870 --> 00:23:44.950 align:middle line:90%
like $50 to $60 billion.

00:23:44.950 --> 00:23:47.630 align:middle line:84%
CME'S volume is
like a quadrillion.

00:23:47.630 --> 00:23:50.190 align:middle line:90%
It's just not even comparable.

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:52.390 align:middle line:90%
But Coinbase charges a lot more.

00:23:52.390 --> 00:23:56.630 align:middle line:84%
They charge you a lot
more per dollar traded.

00:23:56.630 --> 00:23:58.970 align:middle line:84%
And I think we see
that same balance,

00:23:58.970 --> 00:24:02.550 align:middle line:84%
and I think that
it's interesting.

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:08.470 align:middle line:84%
The thing that we see, and
I see this trend continuing

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:10.110 align:middle line:84%
in the next few
years, especially

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:14.230 align:middle line:84%
with the Trump
administration, there's

00:24:14.230 --> 00:24:18.460 align:middle line:90%
a very strong appetite for new--

00:24:18.460 --> 00:24:21.980 align:middle line:84%
new financial markets
and new media.

00:24:21.980 --> 00:24:23.920 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of
distrust in the system,

00:24:23.920 --> 00:24:25.880 align:middle line:84%
both in the Wall Street
and financial markets

00:24:25.880 --> 00:24:26.940 align:middle line:90%
but also media.

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:29.380 align:middle line:84%
People just don't really
believe the pundits

00:24:29.380 --> 00:24:32.180 align:middle line:90%
and traditional authorities.

00:24:32.180 --> 00:24:35.660 align:middle line:84%
And I think prediction markets
are very uniquely suited to be

00:24:35.660 --> 00:24:39.180 align:middle line:90%
at the intersection of both.

00:24:39.180 --> 00:24:41.800 align:middle line:90%
That's a retail-first market.

00:24:41.800 --> 00:24:43.780 align:middle line:84%
That's retail first, and
then the institutions

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:45.447 align:middle line:84%
follow, rather than
an institution first

00:24:45.447 --> 00:24:49.580 align:middle line:84%
and then figure out how to get
the retail to adopt essentially.

00:24:49.580 --> 00:24:50.640 align:middle line:90%
And we're seeing that.

00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:53.025 align:middle line:90%
We're seeing that.

00:24:53.025 --> 00:24:53.900 align:middle line:90%
VASILY STRELA: Right.

00:24:53.900 --> 00:24:54.840 align:middle line:90%
Very interesting.

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:56.700 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:24:56.700 --> 00:24:58.293 align:middle line:90%
TAREK MANSOUR: Steven.

00:24:58.293 --> 00:25:00.460 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Yeah, I have a
question about-- because you

00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:04.440 align:middle line:84%
said that you were trying to
also get into sports as well.

00:25:04.440 --> 00:25:10.580 align:middle line:84%
So I was wondering if
you planned on offering--

00:25:10.580 --> 00:25:13.770 align:middle line:84%
or similar things to other
sports books and stuff,

00:25:13.770 --> 00:25:17.230 align:middle line:84%
like traditional bets, like
money lines and stuff like that,

00:25:17.230 --> 00:25:21.610 align:middle line:84%
or would it be more of the
work, more like a broader

00:25:21.610 --> 00:25:24.060 align:middle line:90%
prediction-type sports bets?

00:25:24.060 --> 00:25:30.090 align:middle line:90%


00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:31.690 align:middle line:84%
TAREK MANSOUR: That's
a good question.

00:25:31.690 --> 00:25:34.690 align:middle line:84%
Both basically is
the way I put it.

00:25:34.690 --> 00:25:37.770 align:middle line:90%
I think both.

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:40.010 align:middle line:84%
The prediction stuff
is the one that's

00:25:40.010 --> 00:25:42.608 align:middle line:84%
more interesting because that's
the one that people in news

00:25:42.608 --> 00:25:43.150 align:middle line:90%
thinks about.

00:25:43.150 --> 00:25:45.210 align:middle line:84%
What are some of
the big things that

00:25:45.210 --> 00:25:48.750 align:middle line:84%
are going to happen in the
next few months or years?

00:25:48.750 --> 00:25:51.092 align:middle line:84%
And then the money line
is just very retentive.

00:25:51.092 --> 00:25:53.550 align:middle line:84%
It's, like, people come back,
and they do it over and over,

00:25:53.550 --> 00:25:55.092 align:middle line:84%
and then they engage
with the product

00:25:55.092 --> 00:25:56.910 align:middle line:84%
meaningfully in a
very consistent way.

00:25:56.910 --> 00:25:59.388 align:middle line:84%
So I think that, yeah, I
think the answer is both.

00:25:59.388 --> 00:26:01.430 align:middle line:84%
We have some regulatory
work to do on that front,

00:26:01.430 --> 00:26:03.933 align:middle line:84%
but the next administration
is going to be very favorable.

00:26:03.933 --> 00:26:06.350 align:middle line:84%
I mean, Trump himself has been
courting our markets a lot.

00:26:06.350 --> 00:26:06.990 align:middle line:90%
He loves the market.

00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:07.870 align:middle line:90%
He loves the company.

00:26:07.870 --> 00:26:12.123 align:middle line:84%
I think he's going to see a big
shift from an administration

00:26:12.123 --> 00:26:14.790 align:middle line:84%
that was somewhat anti-financial
innovation to an administration

00:26:14.790 --> 00:26:17.370 align:middle line:84%
that is going to be very,
very pro-financial innovation

00:26:17.370 --> 00:26:20.270 align:middle line:84%
and prediction
markets specifically.

00:26:20.270 --> 00:26:20.770 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: OK.

00:26:20.770 --> 00:26:23.010 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, that makes sense.

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:26.570 align:middle line:84%
JAKE XIA: So Tarek, I'm
throwing you another question.

00:26:26.570 --> 00:26:29.810 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if you can
talk about the revenue

00:26:29.810 --> 00:26:30.750 align:middle line:90%
of the company.

00:26:30.750 --> 00:26:33.610 align:middle line:84%
Is the company
profitable so far?

00:26:33.610 --> 00:26:36.910 align:middle line:84%
The revenue model is
from a commission model,

00:26:36.910 --> 00:26:40.250 align:middle line:84%
just to give students
some idea how--

00:26:40.250 --> 00:26:44.480 align:middle line:84%
why the investors are interested
in owning the company.

00:26:44.480 --> 00:26:47.930 align:middle line:90%


00:26:47.930 --> 00:26:51.250 align:middle line:84%
TAREK MANSOUR: The company can
be profitable if you want to,

00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:54.730 align:middle line:90%
but we don't right now.

00:26:54.730 --> 00:26:57.910 align:middle line:84%
And oftentimes, it's a little
bit of a counterintuitive thing.

00:26:57.910 --> 00:27:02.610 align:middle line:84%
But I think increasing
the growth at this stage

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:05.020 align:middle line:84%
is more important than
profitability in a lot of cases.

00:27:05.020 --> 00:27:07.503 align:middle line:90%


00:27:07.503 --> 00:27:09.170 align:middle line:84%
And the reason why
exchanges-- exchanges

00:27:09.170 --> 00:27:10.380 align:middle line:90%
are very popular businesses.

00:27:10.380 --> 00:27:11.565 align:middle line:90%
They're very hard to set up.

00:27:11.565 --> 00:27:13.940 align:middle line:84%
It's unbelievably hard to get
an exchange up and running.

00:27:13.940 --> 00:27:15.400 align:middle line:84%
It's just really,
really difficult.

00:27:15.400 --> 00:27:17.300 align:middle line:84%
But when it's up and running
and you have liquidity,

00:27:17.300 --> 00:27:19.140 align:middle line:84%
it's actually one of the
best form of businesses.

00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:20.960 align:middle line:84%
It's some of the most
profitable businesses

00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:24.440 align:middle line:90%
are exchanges actually.

00:27:24.440 --> 00:27:28.460 align:middle line:84%
And so right now the strategy
is basically hardcore expansion.

00:27:28.460 --> 00:27:30.060 align:middle line:90%
Just grow, grow, grow, grow.

00:27:30.060 --> 00:27:30.780 align:middle line:90%
Go mainstream.

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:32.863 align:middle line:90%
Get people used to the asset.

00:27:32.863 --> 00:27:34.280 align:middle line:84%
And we're making
revenue, but it's

00:27:34.280 --> 00:27:36.880 align:middle line:84%
just like we're reinvesting it
all into marketing and growth.

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:39.920 align:middle line:84%
And then over time, we're
basically-- as we taper,

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:42.680 align:middle line:84%
and as we get to even
larger scale basically,

00:27:42.680 --> 00:27:44.372 align:middle line:90%
turn on the profit machine.

00:27:44.372 --> 00:27:46.080 align:middle line:84%
And the source of
revenue is essentially,

00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:50.540 align:middle line:84%
like, we make money
on trading fees,

00:27:50.540 --> 00:27:52.138 align:middle line:84%
like CME or New
York Stock Exchange.

00:27:52.138 --> 00:27:53.680 align:middle line:84%
You take a fee off
every transaction.

00:27:53.680 --> 00:27:56.320 align:middle line:84%
If you put $100 in
cash, you take $1 to $2.

00:27:56.320 --> 00:27:59.057 align:middle line:84%
And then on market making,
so we have a separate arm,

00:27:59.057 --> 00:28:01.140 align:middle line:84%
a bit like Citadel market
making, on this product.

00:28:01.140 --> 00:28:03.420 align:middle line:84%
And they're already one of
the prime market makers.

00:28:03.420 --> 00:28:04.520 align:middle line:84%
They have competition,
so they compete

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:07.080 align:middle line:84%
a lot against SIG, which is
one of our major partners.

00:28:07.080 --> 00:28:10.950 align:middle line:84%
And then there's a few other
prop shops or market making,

00:28:10.950 --> 00:28:13.483 align:middle line:90%
like hedge funds in the firm.

00:28:13.483 --> 00:28:15.150 align:middle line:84%
And then, three, over
time, right now we

00:28:15.150 --> 00:28:16.530 align:middle line:84%
make it all free
and open source,

00:28:16.530 --> 00:28:18.350 align:middle line:90%
but the data is very valuable.

00:28:18.350 --> 00:28:20.350 align:middle line:84%
We have a lot of very
valuable data about trends

00:28:20.350 --> 00:28:22.310 align:middle line:90%
and where the world is going.

00:28:22.310 --> 00:28:25.553 align:middle line:84%
And there's a lot of
demand to get the data.

00:28:25.553 --> 00:28:27.470 align:middle line:84%
But for now, we're just
making it open source,

00:28:27.470 --> 00:28:28.250 align:middle line:90%
so anyone can use it.

00:28:28.250 --> 00:28:30.030 align:middle line:84%
Anyone can make use of it,
so over time it can basically

00:28:30.030 --> 00:28:31.530 align:middle line:90%
capture value in that as well.

00:28:31.530 --> 00:28:34.470 align:middle line:90%


00:28:34.470 --> 00:28:36.750 align:middle line:84%
JAKE XIA: Just a
follow up on that.

00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:41.350 align:middle line:84%
You mentioned Citadel,
SIG, those market makers.

00:28:41.350 --> 00:28:45.750 align:middle line:84%
How do you incentivize
them to come to Kalshi?

00:28:45.750 --> 00:28:51.030 align:middle line:84%
And are there any requirements
of how tight the bid offer

00:28:51.030 --> 00:28:53.550 align:middle line:84%
spread they need to
make when they make

00:28:53.550 --> 00:28:56.037 align:middle line:90%
market on certain contracts?

00:28:56.037 --> 00:28:56.870 align:middle line:90%
TAREK MANSOUR: Yeah.

00:28:56.870 --> 00:28:58.620 align:middle line:84%
So, no, there are
definitely requirements.

00:28:58.620 --> 00:28:59.770 align:middle line:90%
So I'll give an example.

00:28:59.770 --> 00:29:01.353 align:middle line:84%
In the election
market, they had to be

00:29:01.353 --> 00:29:07.100 align:middle line:84%
$0.01 spread with $1 million
on each side at least.

00:29:07.100 --> 00:29:09.920 align:middle line:84%
And it was funny because Jeff
Yass, the founder of SIG,

00:29:09.920 --> 00:29:12.500 align:middle line:84%
actually texted
Elon and asked him

00:29:12.500 --> 00:29:14.020 align:middle line:84%
if he wanted to
do a $100 million

00:29:14.020 --> 00:29:17.740 align:middle line:84%
trade on Kalshi
during the election.

00:29:17.740 --> 00:29:20.100 align:middle line:84%
But Elon was too concerned
of conflict of interest

00:29:20.100 --> 00:29:22.115 align:middle line:90%
and a bunch of things.

00:29:22.115 --> 00:29:23.740 align:middle line:84%
And they could have
done it with $0.01,

00:29:23.740 --> 00:29:25.400 align:middle line:84%
so the spreads were
very, very tight.

00:29:25.400 --> 00:29:26.380 align:middle line:84%
I'm saying this
because they could

00:29:26.380 --> 00:29:28.380 align:middle line:84%
have done-- they could
do up to $100 million

00:29:28.380 --> 00:29:32.121 align:middle line:84%
within like $0.01 slippage,
which is really awesome.

00:29:32.121 --> 00:29:34.204 align:middle line:84%
And so, yes, there are
requirements around spread.

00:29:34.204 --> 00:29:37.260 align:middle line:90%


00:29:37.260 --> 00:29:40.860 align:middle line:84%
I think the way we think about
the ecosystem needs to be--

00:29:40.860 --> 00:29:47.080 align:middle line:84%
or the way that
it's structured, you

00:29:47.080 --> 00:29:49.080 align:middle line:84%
give some incentives at
the beginning of markets

00:29:49.080 --> 00:29:50.880 align:middle line:84%
so that after that, you
basically let it do.

00:29:50.880 --> 00:29:51.900 align:middle line:84%
And the incentive's
basically there.

00:29:51.900 --> 00:29:52.920 align:middle line:90%
They're getting flow.

00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:54.480 align:middle line:84%
They're trading against
a bunch of other people,

00:29:54.480 --> 00:29:57.180 align:middle line:84%
and then they hope to make
money market making and doing--

00:29:57.180 --> 00:29:59.100 align:middle line:84%
getting bid at
spread essentially.

00:29:59.100 --> 00:30:01.980 align:middle line:84%
And so that's our sort of,
I would say, the structure.

00:30:01.980 --> 00:30:05.700 align:middle line:84%
And it's pretty common structure
in the traditional markets.

00:30:05.700 --> 00:30:08.960 align:middle line:84%
I think your question is good
because the stock exchanges,

00:30:08.960 --> 00:30:11.188 align:middle line:84%
they do a lot of
incentive because stocks--

00:30:11.188 --> 00:30:12.480 align:middle line:90%
there's a lot of stock markets.

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:13.460 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of
stock exchanges.

00:30:13.460 --> 00:30:15.620 align:middle line:84%
They're very competitive with
each other because of the way

00:30:15.620 --> 00:30:16.980 align:middle line:90%
that the SEC has set up rules.

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:18.920 align:middle line:84%
In the CFC world, there's
not a lot of competition.

00:30:18.920 --> 00:30:20.740 align:middle line:84%
It's actually very
monopolistic in nature.

00:30:20.740 --> 00:30:22.020 align:middle line:90%
Each one has their--

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:23.780 align:middle line:84%
CME has their
interest rate swaps

00:30:23.780 --> 00:30:26.053 align:middle line:84%
and S&P. ICE has all
the energy stuff,

00:30:26.053 --> 00:30:27.220 align:middle line:90%
and I think we may be able--

00:30:27.220 --> 00:30:28.595 align:middle line:84%
I think it looks
like we're going

00:30:28.595 --> 00:30:32.260 align:middle line:84%
to be able to dominate anything
that's event-related over time.

00:30:32.260 --> 00:30:35.860 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So I'm just
intrigued as to how you've

00:30:35.860 --> 00:30:39.780 align:middle line:84%
gone from just working in a
company like Citadel, Goldman,

00:30:39.780 --> 00:30:44.300 align:middle line:84%
to just having this idea of
this exchange and actually just

00:30:44.300 --> 00:30:45.520 align:middle line:90%
being able to make it.

00:30:45.520 --> 00:30:48.340 align:middle line:84%
Because to me, it seems like
even if I had an idea of,

00:30:48.340 --> 00:30:51.980 align:middle line:84%
oh, this would be a great thing
to turn into an exchange, just

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:54.580 align:middle line:84%
the idea of actually
making that happen

00:30:54.580 --> 00:30:57.900 align:middle line:90%
seems really hard to achieve.

00:30:57.900 --> 00:31:00.780 align:middle line:84%
From the idea to
actually getting it

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:05.470 align:middle line:84%
into a reasonable
product, how did you--

00:31:05.470 --> 00:31:06.470 align:middle line:90%
what did you have to do?

00:31:06.470 --> 00:31:08.290 align:middle line:84%
Did you just have
to literally start

00:31:08.290 --> 00:31:10.330 align:middle line:84%
emailing people in
the industry, and then

00:31:10.330 --> 00:31:12.570 align:middle line:90%
start working your way up?

00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:13.510 align:middle line:90%
How did that work?

00:31:13.510 --> 00:31:15.250 align:middle line:90%
TAREK MANSOUR: Yeah.

00:31:15.250 --> 00:31:17.583 align:middle line:90%
So yes, that's it.

00:31:17.583 --> 00:31:18.750 align:middle line:90%
I mean, that's literally it.

00:31:18.750 --> 00:31:24.730 align:middle line:90%


00:31:24.730 --> 00:31:26.588 align:middle line:84%
I'll say a few things
about this because I

00:31:26.588 --> 00:31:27.630 align:middle line:90%
thought about this a lot.

00:31:27.630 --> 00:31:33.090 align:middle line:84%
So it was a tough decision to
leave Citadel, like when I--

00:31:33.090 --> 00:31:35.730 align:middle line:84%
and I think, Peter, you and I
had a conversation about this

00:31:35.730 --> 00:31:36.230 align:middle line:90%
as well.

00:31:36.230 --> 00:31:38.680 align:middle line:90%
I think at the time, we--

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:41.210 align:middle line:90%


00:31:41.210 --> 00:31:44.170 align:middle line:90%
Citadel pays a lot of money.

00:31:44.170 --> 00:31:46.270 align:middle line:84%
Even as a first
timer, it's crazy.

00:31:46.270 --> 00:31:48.567 align:middle line:84%
And so I didn't come from a
very privileged background,

00:31:48.567 --> 00:31:50.650 align:middle line:84%
so I remember I had a
conversation with my parents

00:31:50.650 --> 00:31:52.710 align:middle line:84%
like, hey, I wanted
to start this company.

00:31:52.710 --> 00:31:53.670 align:middle line:84%
They're like, oh,
what is it about?

00:31:53.670 --> 00:31:55.837 align:middle line:84%
I was like, oh, this thing
called prediction market.

00:31:55.837 --> 00:31:58.450 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, they had no idea
what I was talking about.

00:31:58.450 --> 00:32:00.450 align:middle line:84%
And I was like, I mean,
there's another problem.

00:32:00.450 --> 00:32:02.760 align:middle line:84%
So it's illegal in the US
now, and I want to do this.

00:32:02.760 --> 00:32:06.072 align:middle line:84%
And they're like, OK, and so
you have to leave your job?

00:32:06.072 --> 00:32:06.780 align:middle line:90%
I was like, yeah.

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:08.220 align:middle line:84%
And then they
obviously got inflamed.

00:32:08.220 --> 00:32:09.920 align:middle line:84%
They were like, what
are you talking about?

00:32:09.920 --> 00:32:11.462 align:middle line:84%
These guys are paying
all this money.

00:32:11.462 --> 00:32:13.360 align:middle line:90%
Well, does that make any sense?

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:16.840 align:middle line:84%
And I think the thing
that pushed us to do

00:32:16.840 --> 00:32:19.728 align:middle line:90%
it is, I think I kind of--

00:32:19.728 --> 00:32:22.100 align:middle line:84%
some people make decisions
based on regret minimization.

00:32:22.100 --> 00:32:25.760 align:middle line:84%
It's like, in 10 years, what
path would I regret the most?

00:32:25.760 --> 00:32:28.253 align:middle line:84%
And I just thought I would
regret if I hadn't done it.

00:32:28.253 --> 00:32:29.920 align:middle line:84%
I just thought I would
be very regretful

00:32:29.920 --> 00:32:32.440 align:middle line:84%
if I didn't do this, or at
least try or give it a shot.

00:32:32.440 --> 00:32:34.640 align:middle line:90%
And so I just had to do it.

00:32:34.640 --> 00:32:36.700 align:middle line:84%
And then I really
liked the idea.

00:32:36.700 --> 00:32:38.320 align:middle line:90%
I just really love--

00:32:38.320 --> 00:32:40.680 align:middle line:84%
I love the idea of
basically creating

00:32:40.680 --> 00:32:42.520 align:middle line:84%
a market where you
can price anything

00:32:42.520 --> 00:32:44.645 align:middle line:84%
and making the world just
smarter about the future.

00:32:44.645 --> 00:32:47.240 align:middle line:90%


00:32:47.240 --> 00:32:49.380 align:middle line:84%
So then, OK, what
do we actually do?

00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:52.680 align:middle line:90%


00:32:52.680 --> 00:32:59.000 align:middle line:84%
The simplest way
to describe what

00:32:59.000 --> 00:33:02.018 align:middle line:84%
it takes to build a company, so
Mark Andreessen has this quote--

00:33:02.018 --> 00:33:04.060 align:middle line:84%
I forgot if it's Mark
Andreessen or Ben Horowitz,

00:33:04.060 --> 00:33:06.760 align:middle line:90%
one of the two a16z guys--

00:33:06.760 --> 00:33:10.040 align:middle line:84%
about how when he talks
about-- to a lot of CEOs,

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:14.680 align:middle line:84%
he basically has this whole
thing about all the pretty--

00:33:14.680 --> 00:33:16.640 align:middle line:84%
the good CEOs usually
respond with, OK.

00:33:16.640 --> 00:33:18.280 align:middle line:84%
When he asked them,
How did you do it?

00:33:18.280 --> 00:33:23.700 align:middle line:84%
they say, it was a great
strategy, great execution.

00:33:23.700 --> 00:33:25.180 align:middle line:84%
We had all these
great decisions,

00:33:25.180 --> 00:33:26.680 align:middle line:84%
blah, blah, blah,
blah, blah, blah.

00:33:26.680 --> 00:33:30.200 align:middle line:84%
And then he says, the absolute
great CEOs, usually their answer

00:33:30.200 --> 00:33:33.720 align:middle line:90%
is, we didn't give up.

00:33:33.720 --> 00:33:36.360 align:middle line:84%
We just pushed harder
than anyone else.

00:33:36.360 --> 00:33:38.540 align:middle line:84%
And I think there's a
lot of truth to that.

00:33:38.540 --> 00:33:41.540 align:middle line:84%
I really do, because when
you're young, people say,

00:33:41.540 --> 00:33:44.040 align:middle line:84%
well, when you're young, you
have to wait and get experience

00:33:44.040 --> 00:33:44.923 align:middle line:90%
to build a company.

00:33:44.923 --> 00:33:46.340 align:middle line:84%
And there's pros
and cons to both.

00:33:46.340 --> 00:33:47.280 align:middle line:84%
When you're too
experienced, I think

00:33:47.280 --> 00:33:48.960 align:middle line:84%
it's harder to be
a little crazier

00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:51.840 align:middle line:90%
and do bolder, crazier ideas.

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:54.700 align:middle line:84%
But when you're younger, you
don't know much about anything,

00:33:54.700 --> 00:33:55.220 align:middle line:90%
really.

00:33:55.220 --> 00:33:56.720 align:middle line:84%
You don't know how
to manage people.

00:33:56.720 --> 00:33:58.910 align:middle line:84%
You don't know how
to raise money.

00:33:58.910 --> 00:33:59.910 align:middle line:90%
You don't know anything.

00:33:59.910 --> 00:34:01.130 align:middle line:90%
And you just have to stumble.

00:34:01.130 --> 00:34:06.350 align:middle line:84%
You just have to run and fall,
and then stand up, and run

00:34:06.350 --> 00:34:07.850 align:middle line:90%
and fall again, and stand back.

00:34:07.850 --> 00:34:08.949 align:middle line:84%
And then you do it enough
times, and then you

00:34:08.949 --> 00:34:10.170 align:middle line:90%
get used to falling.

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:11.070 align:middle line:84%
And then you getting
used to falling

00:34:11.070 --> 00:34:13.530 align:middle line:84%
is actually one of the best
superpowers you can ever have.

00:34:13.530 --> 00:34:18.770 align:middle line:84%
Literally, I think the capacity
to fall and stand back up,

00:34:18.770 --> 00:34:21.350 align:middle line:84%
I think is the single best
determinant of the entrepreneurs

00:34:21.350 --> 00:34:22.650 align:middle line:90%
that go furthest, really.

00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:24.989 align:middle line:84%
And you can look
at it even-- you

00:34:24.989 --> 00:34:27.128 align:middle line:84%
don't need to look at
small private companies

00:34:27.128 --> 00:34:28.170 align:middle line:90%
or big private companies.

00:34:28.170 --> 00:34:31.736 align:middle line:84%
You can look at public
massive large companies.

00:34:31.736 --> 00:34:34.150 align:middle line:84%
Just look at the stock
price of Meta and Tesla,

00:34:34.150 --> 00:34:35.429 align:middle line:90%
and see how it goes.

00:34:35.429 --> 00:34:36.929 align:middle line:84%
It's just not
straight to the right.

00:34:36.929 --> 00:34:39.969 align:middle line:84%
It goes to the shitter for a
few years and goes back up,

00:34:39.969 --> 00:34:41.750 align:middle line:90%
and then it repeats.

00:34:41.750 --> 00:34:45.050 align:middle line:84%
And in every cycle, it goes
higher than the prior cycle,

00:34:45.050 --> 00:34:46.030 align:middle line:90%
and they keep going.

00:34:46.030 --> 00:34:49.010 align:middle line:84%
So I think that's
really what it is.

00:34:49.010 --> 00:34:51.270 align:middle line:84%
And when I think
about my younger

00:34:51.270 --> 00:34:53.683 align:middle line:84%
self, what is the type
of advice I would give?

00:34:53.683 --> 00:34:55.820 align:middle line:84%
I think take more
risks, and that's

00:34:55.820 --> 00:34:58.860 align:middle line:84%
one of the things I learned a
lot from my co-founder, Luana.

00:34:58.860 --> 00:35:00.240 align:middle line:90%
She's always been risk-loving.

00:35:00.240 --> 00:35:04.820 align:middle line:84%
She never really, really
worried about much.

00:35:04.820 --> 00:35:06.660 align:middle line:84%
I was more of a
person that would

00:35:06.660 --> 00:35:08.100 align:middle line:84%
think about
optimizing my career,

00:35:08.100 --> 00:35:12.460 align:middle line:84%
and what's the highest
expected value path?

00:35:12.460 --> 00:35:15.720 align:middle line:84%
What I learned over time from
the company and from her is,

00:35:15.720 --> 00:35:17.820 align:middle line:84%
actually, you just need
to take more risks.

00:35:17.820 --> 00:35:19.700 align:middle line:90%
Taking risks is a very good--

00:35:19.700 --> 00:35:21.790 align:middle line:84%
it's a very good
way to live a life,

00:35:21.790 --> 00:35:23.040 align:middle line:90%
because what's the worst case?

00:35:23.040 --> 00:35:24.457 align:middle line:84%
You fail and then
you do it again.

00:35:24.457 --> 00:35:29.060 align:middle line:90%


00:35:29.060 --> 00:35:32.420 align:middle line:84%
I would say number two is,
pick something and just

00:35:32.420 --> 00:35:33.735 align:middle line:90%
stick with it.

00:35:33.735 --> 00:35:34.860 align:middle line:90%
Just try to pick something.

00:35:34.860 --> 00:35:36.693 align:middle line:84%
If you don't know what
it is yet, that's OK.

00:35:36.693 --> 00:35:38.500 align:middle line:84%
Take a few years
to figure that out.

00:35:38.500 --> 00:35:40.180 align:middle line:84%
But once you feel like
you really like and enjoy

00:35:40.180 --> 00:35:41.660 align:middle line:84%
working on something,
you just pick something

00:35:41.660 --> 00:35:42.720 align:middle line:90%
and just stick with it.

00:35:42.720 --> 00:35:47.220 align:middle line:84%
Just go for it and
have conviction.

00:35:47.220 --> 00:35:48.992 align:middle line:84%
And usually, the
results, the results

00:35:48.992 --> 00:35:50.200 align:middle line:90%
end up being pretty positive.

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:51.408 align:middle line:90%
It's a very, very tough road.

00:35:51.408 --> 00:35:53.610 align:middle line:84%
I mean, Professor
Kempthorne can tell you

00:35:53.610 --> 00:35:56.065 align:middle line:84%
about a lot of the stuff we
had to go through to get here.

00:35:56.065 --> 00:35:58.190 align:middle line:84%
It's a very tough road,
but if you have conviction,

00:35:58.190 --> 00:35:59.270 align:middle line:84%
you're excited about
what you're working on,

00:35:59.270 --> 00:36:00.953 align:middle line:84%
I think you can
get very, very far.

00:36:00.953 --> 00:36:01.870 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: That's great.

00:36:01.870 --> 00:36:05.810 align:middle line:84%
So did you ever set in your
mind essentially a stop-loss,

00:36:05.810 --> 00:36:08.545 align:middle line:84%
where you thought, right,
well, if this person--

00:36:08.545 --> 00:36:11.170 align:middle line:84%
I've asked this many people; if
this person says no, maybe it's

00:36:11.170 --> 00:36:12.150 align:middle line:90%
not actually possible?

00:36:12.150 --> 00:36:13.550 align:middle line:84%
Or did you just never
have that in your mind?

00:36:13.550 --> 00:36:14.383 align:middle line:90%
You just kept going.

00:36:14.383 --> 00:36:24.268 align:middle line:90%


00:36:24.268 --> 00:36:25.810 align:middle line:84%
TAREK MANSOUR: Not
really, basically.

00:36:25.810 --> 00:36:29.130 align:middle line:84%
I mean, definitely not
a people-driven thing.

00:36:29.130 --> 00:36:31.610 align:middle line:84%
What I've learned
is, also, a lot

00:36:31.610 --> 00:36:34.530 align:middle line:84%
is like, don't really
listen to authority figures.

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:38.930 align:middle line:84%
You'd be surprised by the extent
to which people just don't

00:36:38.930 --> 00:36:40.250 align:middle line:90%
really know what they're doing.

00:36:40.250 --> 00:36:42.630 align:middle line:84%
And by people, I
mean not average.

00:36:42.630 --> 00:36:47.930 align:middle line:84%
I'm talking the President of the
United States, the Supreme Court

00:36:47.930 --> 00:36:51.843 align:middle line:84%
Judge, Elon Musk,
like Mark Zuckerberg.

00:36:51.843 --> 00:36:54.010 align:middle line:84%
People don't really know
what they're doing, really.

00:36:54.010 --> 00:36:56.010 align:middle line:84%
Everyone is figuring
it out on the fly.

00:36:56.010 --> 00:37:01.410 align:middle line:84%
And it's interesting because if
anyone had the answer of whether

00:37:01.410 --> 00:37:05.010 align:middle line:84%
something is going to be a
multi-billion dollar company--

00:37:05.010 --> 00:37:07.870 align:middle line:84%
sorry about the
noise, by the way.

00:37:07.870 --> 00:37:09.370 align:middle line:84%
If anyone had the
answer if anything

00:37:09.370 --> 00:37:11.930 align:middle line:84%
was going to be a
multi-billion dollar company,

00:37:11.930 --> 00:37:13.690 align:middle line:90%
they would just do it.

00:37:13.690 --> 00:37:14.870 align:middle line:90%
Why didn't they just do it?

00:37:14.870 --> 00:37:17.810 align:middle line:84%
So in a lot of cases, you cannot
really get the answer from

00:37:17.810 --> 00:37:19.010 align:middle line:90%
outside.

00:37:19.010 --> 00:37:19.890 align:middle line:90%
It's interesting.

00:37:19.890 --> 00:37:20.670 align:middle line:90%
You really can't.

00:37:20.670 --> 00:37:22.550 align:middle line:84%
You just have to
have it inside you.

00:37:22.550 --> 00:37:23.530 align:middle line:90%
And you may be wrong.

00:37:23.530 --> 00:37:24.230 align:middle line:90%
That's a risk.

00:37:24.230 --> 00:37:25.673 align:middle line:84%
You may be wrong
and you may fail.

00:37:25.673 --> 00:37:28.090 align:middle line:84%
And it might suck, but that's
the risk that you're taking.

00:37:28.090 --> 00:37:30.640 align:middle line:90%


00:37:30.640 --> 00:37:32.310 align:middle line:84%
But it has to come
from within you.

00:37:32.310 --> 00:37:33.450 align:middle line:84%
It's like, do you have
conviction in this thing,

00:37:33.450 --> 00:37:35.390 align:middle line:84%
and do you believe
it can work one day?

00:37:35.390 --> 00:37:37.910 align:middle line:90%
And if you do, then go for it.

00:37:37.910 --> 00:37:38.910 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: That's amazing.

00:37:38.910 --> 00:37:40.250 align:middle line:90%
Thank you so much.

00:37:40.250 --> 00:37:42.750 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: So, Tarek,
if I could just chime in there.

00:37:42.750 --> 00:37:51.280 align:middle line:84%
I think what Tarek has
is passion, persistence,

00:37:51.280 --> 00:37:52.860 align:middle line:90%
and energy.

00:37:52.860 --> 00:38:00.360 align:middle line:84%
And knowing how hard he worked
and how inspiring he was,

00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:04.800 align:middle line:84%
that inspiration led to
significant investments

00:38:04.800 --> 00:38:07.220 align:middle line:90%
from top people in finance.

00:38:07.220 --> 00:38:12.640 align:middle line:84%
And so I think it's a testament
to those characteristics

00:38:12.640 --> 00:38:13.340 align:middle line:90%
he mentioned.

00:38:13.340 --> 00:38:19.100 align:middle line:84%
Also, it's a great benefit
to have a good partner.

00:38:19.100 --> 00:38:22.040 align:middle line:84%
And Luana Lopez Lara
really, I think,

00:38:22.040 --> 00:38:25.300 align:middle line:90%
is a great partner with Tarek.

00:38:25.300 --> 00:38:27.590 align:middle line:84%
And they've helped each
other achieve this.

00:38:27.590 --> 00:38:31.747 align:middle line:90%


00:38:31.747 --> 00:38:32.580 align:middle line:90%
TAREK MANSOUR: Yeah.

00:38:32.580 --> 00:38:33.540 align:middle line:90%
No, I appreciate it.

00:38:33.540 --> 00:38:35.580 align:middle line:84%
I mean, maybe my last
few parting words,

00:38:35.580 --> 00:38:37.788 align:middle line:84%
unless there's any other
questions, I mean, I think--

00:38:37.788 --> 00:38:41.600 align:middle line:90%


00:38:41.600 --> 00:38:42.460 align:middle line:90%
I was still at MIT.

00:38:42.460 --> 00:38:44.900 align:middle line:84%
I don't know how many of you are
sophomores, freshman, juniors,

00:38:44.900 --> 00:38:45.400 align:middle line:90%
seniors.

00:38:45.400 --> 00:38:47.593 align:middle line:84%
But here's all the stuff
I would tell myself

00:38:47.593 --> 00:38:48.510 align:middle line:90%
if I was still at MIT.

00:38:48.510 --> 00:38:51.230 align:middle line:90%


00:38:51.230 --> 00:38:55.230 align:middle line:84%
I would take more classes like
Peter's class and less classes

00:38:55.230 --> 00:39:00.430 align:middle line:84%
that are purely theoretical and
far removed from practical--

00:39:00.430 --> 00:39:03.590 align:middle line:90%
many practical things.

00:39:03.590 --> 00:39:05.710 align:middle line:84%
I would probably
study a little less

00:39:05.710 --> 00:39:08.030 align:middle line:90%
and have a little bit more fun.

00:39:08.030 --> 00:39:11.750 align:middle line:84%
And I would worry a little bit
less about the optimal career

00:39:11.750 --> 00:39:15.863 align:middle line:84%
paths and just explore
what interests me more.

00:39:15.863 --> 00:39:18.030 align:middle line:84%
It's crazy the extent to
which a lot of these things

00:39:18.030 --> 00:39:21.742 align:middle line:90%
matter much less than you think.

00:39:21.742 --> 00:39:24.088 align:middle line:84%
And a lot of life is just
actually trying things out

00:39:24.088 --> 00:39:25.630 align:middle line:84%
and seeing what
works and just making

00:39:25.630 --> 00:39:28.150 align:middle line:84%
sure you're excited about the
things that you're working on.

00:39:28.150 --> 00:39:31.113 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, if you guys have
any questions, reach me.

00:39:31.113 --> 00:39:32.530 align:middle line:84%
Just please don't
share the email,

00:39:32.530 --> 00:39:34.550 align:middle line:90%
but it's tarek@kalshi.com.

00:39:34.550 --> 00:39:38.110 align:middle line:84%
My first name at the name
of the company dot com.

00:39:38.110 --> 00:39:40.270 align:middle line:84%
Happy to help in any
way, shape, or form.

00:39:40.270 --> 00:39:43.270 align:middle line:84%
But yeah, you know, congrats
to you guys making it to MIT

00:39:43.270 --> 00:39:46.110 align:middle line:84%
and hopefully making
it out of MIT.

00:39:46.110 --> 00:39:47.890 align:middle line:90%
And really excited to be here.

00:39:47.890 --> 00:39:49.270 align:middle line:90%
Thank you for having me.

00:39:49.270 --> 00:39:53.310 align:middle line:84%
JAKE XIA: Tarek, before you go,
I have to ask you this question.

00:39:53.310 --> 00:39:55.910 align:middle line:84%
What was the most
memorable thing

00:39:55.910 --> 00:39:58.610 align:middle line:84%
you learned from this
class when you took it?

00:39:58.610 --> 00:40:02.270 align:middle line:84%
And do you recall
anything interesting

00:40:02.270 --> 00:40:04.950 align:middle line:90%
you heard from this class?

00:40:04.950 --> 00:40:06.450 align:middle line:90%
Advice to the students?

00:40:06.450 --> 00:40:06.950 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:40:06.950 --> 00:40:10.888 align:middle line:90%


00:40:10.888 --> 00:40:13.430 align:middle line:84%
TAREK MANSOUR: I really liked
that every mathematical concept

00:40:13.430 --> 00:40:16.950 align:middle line:84%
had an application
towards finance,

00:40:16.950 --> 00:40:21.790 align:middle line:84%
and I think that was a very
palpable thing essentially.

00:40:21.790 --> 00:40:24.227 align:middle line:84%
It was, a very, very
palpable thing was, OK, well,

00:40:24.227 --> 00:40:26.310 align:middle line:84%
you're kind of learning
this mathematical concept.

00:40:26.310 --> 00:40:27.450 align:middle line:84%
It's pretty cool
in and of itself.

00:40:27.450 --> 00:40:29.033 align:middle line:84%
But then when you
apply it to finance,

00:40:29.033 --> 00:40:31.230 align:middle line:84%
you see there's actual
real practicalities

00:40:31.230 --> 00:40:33.070 align:middle line:90%
to the whole thing.

00:40:33.070 --> 00:40:35.730 align:middle line:90%
And I think that stuck with me.

00:40:35.730 --> 00:40:38.350 align:middle line:84%
I think that because a lot of
the other classes I was taking

00:40:38.350 --> 00:40:41.550 align:middle line:84%
was basically mostly pure
math or kind of very removed

00:40:41.550 --> 00:40:43.700 align:middle line:84%
from industry, whereas
here, I remember

00:40:43.700 --> 00:40:47.048 align:middle line:84%
one of the projects
we had to do was--

00:40:47.048 --> 00:40:48.590 align:middle line:84%
there was this paper
we had to write.

00:40:48.590 --> 00:40:51.820 align:middle line:90%


00:40:51.820 --> 00:40:53.450 align:middle line:90%
And I think the one I wrote--

00:40:53.450 --> 00:40:54.200 align:middle line:90%
it's been a while.

00:40:54.200 --> 00:40:56.320 align:middle line:84%
It's been like, I don't
know now, like six years,

00:40:56.320 --> 00:40:57.140 align:middle line:90%
seven years.

00:40:57.140 --> 00:41:00.853 align:middle line:84%
But the paper I
had to write was,

00:41:00.853 --> 00:41:02.520 align:middle line:84%
we were running some
form of regression.

00:41:02.520 --> 00:41:06.820 align:middle line:84%
It was a complicated
form of regression on--

00:41:06.820 --> 00:41:07.980 align:middle line:90%
now I remember.

00:41:07.980 --> 00:41:11.780 align:middle line:84%
I wrote a paper that was trying
to use reinforcement learning

00:41:11.780 --> 00:41:14.540 align:middle line:90%
to predict the stock market.

00:41:14.540 --> 00:41:17.040 align:middle line:84%
And it was, I think, our
final paper of the class,

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:19.920 align:middle line:90%
and it was pretty hard.

00:41:19.920 --> 00:41:24.140 align:middle line:84%
I don't think our model
worked, as you would expect,

00:41:24.140 --> 00:41:25.920 align:middle line:84%
but it was really
interesting just trying.

00:41:25.920 --> 00:41:28.868 align:middle line:84%
I think that was very useful
after when I went into trading.

00:41:28.868 --> 00:41:30.660 align:middle line:84%
People would be talking
about Markov chains

00:41:30.660 --> 00:41:32.535 align:middle line:84%
and how they wanted to
apply them at Citadel,

00:41:32.535 --> 00:41:34.260 align:middle line:84%
and I had already
done that in the class

00:41:34.260 --> 00:41:36.120 align:middle line:90%
and had already applied them.

00:41:36.120 --> 00:41:39.052 align:middle line:84%
And when I used
it at Citadel, it

00:41:39.052 --> 00:41:40.760 align:middle line:84%
was like, holy shit,
this guy's a genius.

00:41:40.760 --> 00:41:42.250 align:middle line:84%
I'm like, you don't
know anything.

00:41:42.250 --> 00:41:44.850 align:middle line:90%
At MIT people do this every day.

00:41:44.850 --> 00:41:46.853 align:middle line:84%
And that was the next MIT
guy, by the way, who's

00:41:46.853 --> 00:41:48.770 align:middle line:84%
now-- you should Google
him, Jon Tipermas He's

00:41:48.770 --> 00:41:50.270 align:middle line:84%
one of the most
successful portfolio

00:41:50.270 --> 00:41:54.100 align:middle line:90%
managers on Wall Street.

00:41:54.100 --> 00:41:58.690 align:middle line:84%
He's a G. This desk is one
of the best performing desks

00:41:58.690 --> 00:42:00.170 align:middle line:90%
at Citadel.

00:42:00.170 --> 00:42:02.490 align:middle line:84%
The reports is that apparently
they made $2 billion

00:42:02.490 --> 00:42:05.370 align:middle line:84%
in 2022 alone, and they've
been making more since then.

00:42:05.370 --> 00:42:07.203 align:middle line:84%
And they were impressed
by some of the stuff

00:42:07.203 --> 00:42:11.090 align:middle line:84%
that I learned in this class
that I was applying on the desk.

00:42:11.090 --> 00:42:14.610 align:middle line:84%
I think this goes to the broader
thing that I was mentioning.

00:42:14.610 --> 00:42:16.970 align:middle line:84%
I think it's just
good to be practical,

00:42:16.970 --> 00:42:18.350 align:middle line:90%
just very good to be practical.

00:42:18.350 --> 00:42:20.143 align:middle line:84%
I get very, very
smart, but also be

00:42:20.143 --> 00:42:21.810 align:middle line:84%
practical because a
lot of the world is.

00:42:21.810 --> 00:42:23.570 align:middle line:90%
And you see it like--

00:42:23.570 --> 00:42:26.650 align:middle line:84%
like an example,
the people that have

00:42:26.650 --> 00:42:30.730 align:middle line:84%
run the largest companies,
even the largest hedge funds,

00:42:30.730 --> 00:42:32.070 align:middle line:90%
are not exactly the smartest.

00:42:32.070 --> 00:42:33.888 align:middle line:84%
Maybe Jim Simons
is an exception,

00:42:33.888 --> 00:42:35.430 align:middle line:84%
but they're not
exactly the smartest.

00:42:35.430 --> 00:42:37.728 align:middle line:84%
LTCM blew up, and those
were the smartest guys.

00:42:37.728 --> 00:42:41.518 align:middle line:90%


00:42:41.518 --> 00:42:44.060 align:middle line:84%
A lot of the others, like Steve
Cohen and some of the others,

00:42:44.060 --> 00:42:47.022 align:middle line:84%
they're pretty smart, but
they're more well-rounded.

00:42:47.022 --> 00:42:48.480 align:middle line:84%
They're just much
more well-rounded

00:42:48.480 --> 00:42:50.280 align:middle line:84%
than just being
the best at math.

00:42:50.280 --> 00:42:52.500 align:middle line:84%
And I think there's a
lot of learning to that.

00:42:52.500 --> 00:42:55.640 align:middle line:90%


00:42:55.640 --> 00:42:56.440 align:middle line:90%
JAKE XIA: Great.

00:42:56.440 --> 00:42:58.255 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:42:58.255 --> 00:42:59.380 align:middle line:90%
TAREK MANSOUR: Well, great.

00:42:59.380 --> 00:42:59.922 align:middle line:90%
Well, thanks.

00:42:59.922 --> 00:43:01.600 align:middle line:84%
Thanks so much for
having me, everyone.

00:43:01.600 --> 00:43:03.100 align:middle line:84%
And hopefully we'll
catch up soon.

00:43:03.100 --> 00:43:04.100 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Great.

00:43:04.100 --> 00:43:06.098 align:middle line:90%
Thank you very much, Tarek.

00:43:06.098 --> 00:43:07.140 align:middle line:90%
VASILY STRELA: Thank you.

00:43:07.140 --> 00:43:10.160 align:middle line:90%


00:43:10.160 --> 00:43:13.120 align:middle line:84%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: But
I think his comments

00:43:13.120 --> 00:43:19.960 align:middle line:84%
about basically having passion
and persistence and energy,

00:43:19.960 --> 00:43:25.100 align:middle line:84%
you can really achieve many
things and achieve your dreams.

00:43:25.100 --> 00:43:32.520 align:middle line:84%
So I do want to encourage you
all to take stock of that.

00:43:32.520 --> 00:43:36.680 align:middle line:84%
And just as a general
comment about my career,

00:43:36.680 --> 00:43:42.000 align:middle line:84%
I started off in academics and
then found consulting projects

00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:45.260 align:middle line:84%
to be very stimulating
and engaging.

00:43:45.260 --> 00:43:50.160 align:middle line:84%
And I decided to leave academics
and start my own company,

00:43:50.160 --> 00:43:53.440 align:middle line:84%
and ended up starting
a couple of hedge funds

00:43:53.440 --> 00:43:58.520 align:middle line:84%
and then doing investment
management for 20 years.

00:43:58.520 --> 00:44:03.840 align:middle line:84%
Then 10 years ago, I had the
opportunity to come back to MIT

00:44:03.840 --> 00:44:07.720 align:middle line:84%
and teach, and I've been
savoring that experience

00:44:07.720 --> 00:44:08.340 align:middle line:90%
as well.

00:44:08.340 --> 00:44:12.000 align:middle line:84%
So don't be afraid of
changes in your life

00:44:12.000 --> 00:44:14.400 align:middle line:90%
and changing your paths.

00:44:14.400 --> 00:44:19.660 align:middle line:84%
And taking risks leads
to great opportunities.

00:44:19.660 --> 00:44:28.880 align:middle line:84%
So hopefully you'll all benefit
from that kind of perspective.

00:44:28.880 --> 00:44:30.670 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:44:30.670 --> 00:44:37.000 align:middle line:90%